# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1 has nothing from it; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.3 have "heat_varies → heat_not_in_matter" and "heat_varies"; they differ when only "the same body appears cold to one hand and warm to another" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1, to: Suppose "the same body appears cold to one hand and warm to another" is true and "heat and cold are affections only of the mind, not patterns of real beings existing in corporeal substances" is false. Is S2, "Thus, for instance, it is said that heat and cold are affections only of the mind, and not at all patterns of real beings, existing in the corporeal substances which excite them, for that the same body which appears cold to one hand seems warm to another.", true then?
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1 has nothing from it; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.3 have "sweet_varies → sweet_not_in_matter" and "sweet_varies"; they differ when only "the thing remaining unaltered, its sweetness is changed into bitter, as with a fevered or vitiated palate" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the thing remaining unaltered, its sweetness is changed into bitter, as with a fevered or vitiated palate" is true and "sweetness is not really in the sapid thing" is false. Is S4, "Again, it is proved that SWEETNESS is not really in the sapid thing, because the thing remaining unaltered the sweetness is changed into bitter, as in case of a fever or otherwise vitiated palate.", true then?
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1 and berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.3 have "ext_not_in_matter ∧ motion_not_in_matter"; berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2 has "heat_not_in_matter ∧ sweet_not_in_matter ∧ ext_not_in_matter ∧ motion_not_in_matter"; they differ when only "heat and cold are affections only of the mind, not patterns of real beings existing in corporeal substances", "figure and extension are not patterns or resemblances of qualities existing in Matter, settled and determinate without the mind" and "motion is not without the mind" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "heat and cold are affections only of the mind, not patterns of real beings existing in corporeal substances", "figure and extension are not patterns or resemblances of qualities existing in Matter, settled and determinate without the mind" and "motion is not without the mind" are true and "sweetness is not really in the sapid thing" is false. Is the conclusion, "A THIRD ARGUMENT AD HOMINEM.--I shall farther add, that, after the same manner as modern philosophers prove certain sensible qualities to have no existence in Matter, or without the mind, the same thing may be likewise proved of all other sensible qualities whatsoever.", true then? [in 1 of 2 runs]
berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1: argue/answers-split: no reading agrees with all 3 answers (berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1 1, berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.2 2, berkeley-principles-1.logic.r1.3 1 of them): each is right somewhere, and the reading the answers want takes statements from more than one
